WASHINGTON, DC – Congresswoman Carol Shea-Porter (NH-01) has signed a House Resolution condemning the charges against Meriam Ibrahim, a pregnant Sudanese woman who has been sentenced to 100 lashes and death by hanging for apostasy and adultery because she married a Christian man. 
“This is an outrageous violation of human rights,” Shea-Porter said. “We must do everything in our power to provide for the safe release of Meriam Ibrahim. I have already sent a letter to U.S. State Department on this issue, and I continue to monitor the situation closely.”
H.Res.601 is a bipartisan resolution expressing that the House of Representatives:
 Condemns the charge of apostasy and adultery of Meriam Ibrahim;
	Calls for the immediate and unconditional release of Ibrahim, her 20-month-old son, and newborn daughter;
	Urges the United States Department of State and the Department of Homeland Security to prioritize granting Ibrahim asylum or refugee status as appropriate; 
	Encourages efforts by the United States Government to support religious freedom within Sudan;
	Recognizes that every individual regardless of religion should have the opportunity to practice his or her religion without fear of discrimination.
 On May 22, Congresswoman Shea-Porter and Senator Jeanne Shaheen (D-NH) sent a letter to Secretary of State John Kerry urging the U.S. State Department to use every means of leverage at its disposal to ensure the release of Meriam Ibrahim. Ms. Ibrahim is married to Daniel Wani, a man originally from Sudan with U.S. citizenship living in New Hampshire.
An identical Senate resolution has been introduced by Senator Marco Rubio (R-FL) and was signed by both New Hampshire Senators. 
