Washington, D.C. – U.S. Senators Susan Collins (R-Maine) and Patty Murray (D-Washington), Ranking Member and Chair of the Senate Appropriations Subcommittee for Transportation, respectively, today introduced a bill that would authorize a new Short Line Rail Safety Institute to enhance the safety practices and culture of short line railroads. There are 550 short line railroad companies that operate over 50,000 miles of track, or nearly one third of the national railroad network.  The tracks can be as short as two miles or up to more than 1,000 miles long.  
                The legislation introduced by Senators Collins and Murray would authorize funding to support grants for research, development, evaluation, and training efforts.                    “The horrific derailment that occurred in Lac-Megantic, Quebec, last year – just 30 miles from the Maine border – brought to light the importance of ensuring the safe transportation of energy products,” Senator Collins said.  “We must ensure that we are taking the necessary steps to prevent another Lac-Megantic, while not overburdening an industry that has a proven track record of safety.”                   “Whether a train is carrying crude oil on a major rail line or on a short, local route through small towns across America, we need to make sure everyone is safe, both on the train and near the tracks,” Senator Murray said.  “We need to have the right policies in place to prevent accidents and respond to emergencies wherever they happen, and establishing a Short Line Rail Safety Institute is a strong step in the right direction.”                   The new Short Line Rail Safety Institute would:

          Assess the operations and safety programs of short line railroads;
         Develop best practices and work with short lines to implement these practices;
         Provide professional on-site safety training  for short line employees;
         Purchase and utilize safety training assets (such as locomotive simulators);
         Assist FRA in implementing its railroad R&amp;D and outreach programs, and tailor such programs for short line railroad operations; and
         Help improve safety culture, including a reduction in the frequency and severity of injuries and incidents, as well as improved compliance with regulatory requirements. 

                On May 15, the two Senators sent a letter to U.S. Department of Transportation Secretary Anthony Foxx expressing support for the creation of such an institution.                    “As we discussed when you testified before our Subcommittee, there is no silver bullet to improving rail safety.  It is a complicated multi-faceted issue involving prevention, mitigation, and response aspects,” the Senators wrote in the May 15 letter.  “The proposed Short Line Railroad Safety Institute could be an important part of the larger solution and would help improve the safe transportation of crude oil and other hazardous materials.”                   Today’s bill follows an April hearing held by Murray and Collins to specifically focus on safety issues related to rail shipment of crude oil.  Secretary Foxx, National Transportation Safety Board Chairman Deborah Hersman, Director of the Seattle Office of Emergency Management Barb Graff, and Rangeley, ME, Fire Chief Tim Pellerin, who led emergency response efforts after a train carrying crude oil derailed in Lac-Megantic, Quebec, in 2013, each testified at that hearing.

On Friday, the House passed the Department of Defense (DoD) Appropriations (H.R. 4870). I voted Yes.
This legislation appropriates $570.4 billion for DoD to carry out its mission and support our remaining troops in Afghanistan. While the legislation is not perfect, overall it is good for our nation, Fort Bliss, our soldiers and their families. Highlights include:
• An amendment I helped author to close loopholes that allow the NSA to conduct surveillance on U.S. citizens without a warrant.
• A 1.8% pay increase for military personnel in 2015 and no increase in Tricare copayments;
• Supports the Tuition Assistance program, which is vital to providing education assistance to Service Members;
• Fully funds sexual assault prevention and response programs and increases funding for military health care programs, including funds to address traumatic brain injuries and psychological health issues.
I am disappointed that H.R. 4870 pushes off tough budgetary decisions and blocks progress in other areas:
• Keeps the Guantanamo Bay Prison (GTMO) open and places restrictions on the President’s ability to transfer detainees to third-party countries;
• Continues funding for unneeded and costly aircraft and weapons systems that DoD has told Congress it doesn’t need;
• Does not address how the United States will responsibly end its military activities in Afghanistan.
I also voted on some important amendments to H.R. 4870. While these amendments did not pass, I think Congress and our country must continue to debate these issues:
• Three amendments offered by Rep. Barbara Lee (CA) that would sunset the Authorization for the Use of Military Force (AUMF) passed after 9/11 so that Congress would need to authorize any new wars in accordance with our Constitutional responsibility. This is especially important considering the escalating sectarian violence in Iraq and renewed pushes to send American troops back to that country.
I am hopeful that the Senate will soon take up this legislation.

